  "body": "`internal/gui/tests/panel-usage.test.cjs` can report `asked: period=today&limit=1` at the final window provider/agent check, although the new window made the correct filtered request.\n\nObserved once in a sequential related-browser run: Chromium zh, Playwright 1.62.1 on Ubuntu Noble (Docker, four CPUs). One isolated baseline rerun passed; this is not an always-red case.\n\nRoot cause: the original panel remains open and its `/api/usage/requests` handler still appends to `asked`. The final `w2` window resets and reuses that same array. `settled` examines only `asked.at(-1)`, so a request from the older panel can replace the window’s correct request as the assertion’s subject.\n\nDeterministic confirmation on the renderer before the balance-percent change (a11d605e): immediately after the existing final `settled` has verified the window’s `provider=relay&agent=codex` request, inject a request from the still-open panel, then repeat that same assertion:\n\n```js\nawait settled(asked, q => q.get(\"provider\") === \"relay\" && q.get(\"agent\") === \"codex\");\nawait p.evaluate(async () => { await fetch(\"/api/usage/requests?period=today&limit=1\"); });\nawait settled(asked, q => q.get(\"provider\") === \"relay\" && q.get(\"agent\") === \"codex\");\n```\n\nThis fails with the identical `asked: period=today&limit=1` message. The first assertion confirms the window’s correct request occurred before the old panel’s request. The request uses the test’s local intercepted fixture, with no live gateway or vendor.\n\nThe small fix should give `w2` its own request log, while leaving the panel open for its later ranking assertion. Increasing the timeout would keep inspecting the wrong page’s request. This report identifies a test-observation race; it does not establish a production filtering failure.\n",
